  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Pipe Efficiency, Diameter, and Equivalent Pipes",
    "description": "Convert relative efficiency and pipe size into comparable flow rates.",
    "teachingApproach": "Connect efficiency to rate: more efficient means more tank per hour and therefore less time for one tank. Build ratio fluency, chained efficiencies, identical-pipe groups, diameter-squared flow, and mixed hot/cold feeder groups. The learner converts every pipe to one common rate unit before combining them.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 16, Pipes and Cisterns. Approved scope: Teach that efficiency is proportional to rate and inversely proportional to time. Cover statements such as one pipe being twice or three times as fast as another, chains such as C twice B and B twice A, and multiple identical pipes. Include the chapter relation that for similar pipes flow is proportional to the square of diameter, so filling/emptying time is inversely proportional to diameter squared. Extend to hot/cold groups and bucket-capacity analogies. Emphasize converting all pipes to one common rate unit before combining them."
  },
